sec.p:
For continuous outcomes (change from baseline), a normal distribution with an identity link was specified. For binomial outcomes (patients experiencing an event) a binomial model with a logit link was specified. All outcomes were analysed using fixed effects and random effects models. Models adjusting for baseline risk were also fitted but generally had poor convergence and were not selected. Where the deviance information criteria were similar (difference of < 5), random effects models were chosen over fixed effects models, as they do not require strong assumptions of homogeneity. Model parameters were estimated using Markov chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) methods (Supplementary Material S1) and vague priors were specified for all basic model parameters, with prior distribution parameters modified according to the scale of the outcome. Details of convergence assessment is provided in the supplementary materials (Supplementary Material S1). Where random effect models showed evidence that the between-study standard deviation τ had not updated after drawing MCMC samples, additional models using informative priors for τ were fitted based on the approach described by Turner et al.: \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
